    <!-- 3. ABOUT THE BOOK                                            -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section id="book" class="relative py-20 lg:py-24 bg-sf-surface" aria-label="About the book">
        <div class="max-w-content mx-auto px-6">
            <div class="animate-on-scroll">
                <div class="accent-line mb-6"></div>
                <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[4px] text-sf-red">About the Book</p>
                <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-5xl md:text-6xl tracking-tight">Spend less time managing architecture. Spend more time delivering value.</h2>
            </div>

            <div class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[1.2fr_0.8fr] gap-10 lg:gap-14 items-start">
                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-300 leading-relaxed animate-on-scroll">Modern software development is often overwhelmed by unnecessary complexity. Teams adopt microservices as the default, layer frameworks upon frameworks, and end up spending more time managing architecture than delivering real value. That complexity leads to fragility, slows development, increases costs, and leaves developers burnt out.</p>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-300 leading-relaxed animate-on-scroll">The Simplicity-First approach is both a toolkit and a practical guide for reversing the trend. Its core idea: software should be simple enough to pass the <span class="text-white font-semibold">2 AM Test</span> &mdash; any developer should be able to understand and fix it under stress.</p>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-300 leading-relaxed animate-on-scroll">Through principles, case studies, and actionable patterns, the book covers the roots of complexity, the trap of designing for edge cases first, modular monoliths over microservice sprawl, scaling intelligently, modernizing legacy systems, the link between simplicity and Green Software, and how to use AI with a human in the loop so it simplifies instead of hiding complexity.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <div class="border border-sf-border rounded-sm bg-sf-black p-7 animate-on-scroll stagger-1">
                        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[3px] text-sf-red">You will learn</p>
                        <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
                            <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sf-red flex-shrink-0">&rsaquo;</span> How to recognize and avoid software complexity traps</li>
                            <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sf-red flex-shrink-0">&rsaquo;</span> Principles like the Half-Rule of Simplicity and the 2 AM Test for guiding design</li>
                            <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sf-red flex-shrink-0">&rsaquo;</span> Practical strategies for modular monoliths, scaling, and legacy modernization</li>
                            <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sf-red flex-shrink-0">&rsaquo;</span> How to align software design with sustainability goals</li>
                            <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sf-red flex-shrink-0">&rsaquo;</span> How to use AI responsibly in architecture without creating black boxes</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="border border-sf-border rounded-sm p-7 animate-on-scroll stagger-2">
                        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[3px] text-sf-muted-dark">Who it&rsquo;s for</p>
                        <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-gray-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                            <li><span class="text-white font-semibold">Developers</span> (intermediate to advanced) frustrated with complexity</li>
                            <li><span class="text-white font-semibold">Solution and enterprise architects</span> balancing business and technology needs</li>
                            <li><span class="text-white font-semibold">Engineering leaders and CTOs</span> who want sustainable, cost-effective architecture</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

